  "reweight_note": "SECONDARY, APPROXIMATE number -- do not treat as a precise clinical incidence rate. The manuscript's headline pooled AUTH drift rate (30.9%) equally weights five synthetic target-CER conditions (0/10/20/30/40%), including CER=0 (no corruption), a condition a real BCI essentially never operates at, and CER=40%, an upper stress-test bound. Reweighting instead by the REAL, selection-count- weighted operating-CER distribution of the 29 bigP3BCI Studies F/L/N participants (2,537 eligible selections, weighted mean CER 0.2136, concentrated near the anchor's published operating point ~0.2136) gives a reweighted AUTH drift estimate of 0.288 (28.8%), versus the naive uniform-grid pooled 0.309 (30.9%). This reweighting is NOT computed from the exposures' own simulated realized_cer (that distribution is ~uniform across 0-0.4 by construction of the experimental design and reweighting by it would be circular); it comes from re-deriving the real per-subject selection-level CER directly from the bigP3BCI EDF source (idrift.data.materialize_confusion.collect_eligible_rows), independent of the simulated corruption pipeline. Caveats: N=29 subjects is small and the resulting weight histogram is lumpy (a handful of subjects set several bins' weight); an unweighted one-subject-one-vote sensitivity version gives 0.306 (30.6%) instead, a materially different number, illustrating that the reweighted point estimate is sensitive to the (defensible but not unique) choice of weighting and should be reported as an approximate, directionally-informative complement to the CER-conditional curve above, not a replacement for it."
